{
  "version": 266,
  "released": "2026-08-30",
  "changelog": "v266: EVENT IDENTITY, PHASE I0 — THE RECORD LAYER: the telling manifest (contract C1) and episode operations plus the binding lifecycle (contract C2). The other half of I0 — C3's fold semantics and C4's merge/split downstream rules — shipped first as v265; the two were built in parallel and merge in either order.\n\nNOTHING IN THIS RELEASE CAN CHANGE A DRAWING, and that is the point. It adds one module, `system/event_identity.py`, that mints records and builds one projection. There is no fold change, no binder, no CLI verb, no work-item kind and no grouping. The controlling design is lifehug-platform `docs/design/event-identity.md` v4 (this repo's half is ADR 0031); its independent auditor's condition for starting work was that authority, identity, migration and recovery semantics be settled in EXECUTABLE contracts before the phases that consume them — so this is those contracts, with eighty-four tests and twelve named fixtures.\n\nWHY ANY OF IT. Executed on the founder's own vault: 881 nodes, 703 unplaced, and Etherfuse alone is six of them — a dated work-history landmark, a dated conversation, and four undated classifier occurrences of the same company. Needs-placing is inflated with facts the person already dated somewhere else, and Play can ask “when did you start Etherfuse?” after they said May 2022. Claim identity is BY SOURCE, the fold groups by `event_ref` and then refuses to guess, and only people are resolved across sources. Nothing in the substrate could say “these are one event”.\n\nC1 — THE TELLING MANIFEST. A *telling* is one source-local account of one event: the classifier's `classification:{stem}#{event_key}` unit, a recorder entry (`landmark:{entry_id}`), or one event inside a promoted message. `TemporalClaim` gains NO FIELD; a claim reaches its telling through what it already carries. `build_telling_manifest` is a pure projection to `state/temporal_claims/telling_manifest.json` over durable inputs only — receipts are immutable and never deleted, so both the old wording and the new one are still on disk — and NOTHING IN IT IS A CLOCK, which is why “delete it and rebuild it byte-identically” is arithmetic and not a hope.\n\nTHE RE-KEY RULE, and it is the whole reason C1 exists. `event_key` is a hash of the MODEL'S OWN title and description, so a re-extraction that rewords either MOVES the identity of a telling nobody corrected. CARDINALITY NEVER ANSWERS THAT. “Exactly one old unmatched event and exactly one new unmatched event” is satisfied just as well by an extraction that dropped one event and discovered a different one, and carrying a confirmed binding across that attaches the wrong story to an episode, silently. So a row re-keys only on an unchanged source LOCATOR recorded in both receipts (spans and turn refs — never the evidence quote, which is the model's transcription and moves when the model does), or a durable recorder-minted event id, or exact agreement on at least TWO independent signature components with ZERO contradictions; the one-candidate condition is a uniqueness GATE on top of that evidence, never evidence itself. Anything weaker preserves BOTH rows and emits a loud `telling_rekey`. A telling that co-existed with the retired one in the same extraction is not a candidate successor at all — it is the other moment that was always there. The four cases live in code as `TELLING_TRANSITIONS`, a table the tests read rather than restate, each with its own fixture.\n\nONE HONEST GAP, CARRIED FORWARD RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER. No extractor declares a document revision yet, and the classifier's claims cite the CLASSIFICATION's revision, which moves whenever the model rewords — so a rewording and a human source correction are currently indistinguishable. The manifest therefore takes the conservative reading, re-keys nothing, and says so by name (`telling_document_revision_undeclared`). `declare_tellings()` is the one call I1 wires into the classifier and the recorder. Failing toward “ask” is the ranking: a miss is cheap, a wrong link is not.\n\nC2 — OPERATIONS AND BINDINGS. An *episode* is an opaque identity minted by an explicit operation receipt and holding no content — the `era_id` pattern from ADR 0030, for the same reason. `operation_id = digest(“eop”, {authority, op, rule_version, member_refs_sorted, acted_on_episode_ids})`: SEMANTIC INPUTS ONLY, no invocation id and no wall clock, so deleting every deterministic identity record and re-running on identical durable inputs reproduces the same operation ids, episode ids and aliases. `episode_id_at_rule_version()` recomputes the id an OLDER rule would have minted, which is how a rule bump lists the old id in `aliases_created` with nothing stored to look it up in. The FIRST human record referencing a deterministic episode adopts it into `sources/identity/`, carrying the creation's own canonical inputs, and from then on no state deletion or rule change orphans what the person acted on.\n\nONE MEMBERSHIP AUTHORITY. Active `event_identity` bindings are the sole grouping authority the fold will ever read; an operation is an atomic transaction-and-audit ENVELOPE naming, by id, every binding it installs and retires. Its `members` list is an audit copy, and a drifted one is a write-time refusal — two truths about membership is the defect this shape exists to prevent. `file_operation_envelope` writes the bindings FIRST and the operation LAST, so a crash inside the mutation seat leaves inert bindings nobody was told about rather than an operation promising records the vault does not hold; and an envelope that names a record which is not there is `identity_envelope_incomplete`, loudly, never a partially applied episode.\n\nSTORAGE IS AUTHORITY. `stated`/`confirmed` bindings and human envelopes live under `sources/identity/`; `deterministic`/`proposed` bindings and rule-authored envelopes live under `state/temporal_claims/identities/`. Deleting `state/` removes what a rule can re-derive and keeps every decision a person made. A `proposed → confirmed` transition files the sources-side record WITH `supersedes` naming the proposal, and an unsuperseded semantic twin across the two directories is a refusal rather than a recency contest — the same reasoning `event_binding.event_resolution_index` already uses. Re-filing the same decision KEEPS the existing bytes and reports; a retry is later, not different.\n\nONE HOME FOR THE VOCABULARY (ADR 0021). `IDENTITY_RULE_VERSION`, `RELATIONS`, `ORIGINS`, `GROUPING_RELATION` and `GROUPING_ORIGINS` belong to v265's `episode_fold_contract` and are IMPORTED here, never restated; v265's one-home sweep is widened by this release from `IDENTITY_RULE_VERSION` alone to all five, scoped to this program's three modules so that `chronology.RELATIONS` — a different word about a different thing — stays none of its business. The record layer adds exactly one value the fold's four world-assertions do not carry, `SPLIT_DEPARTURE_RELATION = \"none\"`, and names it separately rather than growing the shared tuple.\n\nPROVEN TO FIRE. Eighteen guards were each run against a build with that guard removed and SEEN failing before the guard was written — including the one that matters most, the fixture where exactly one telling disappears and exactly one arrives at the same document and they are about completely different things. The three frozen key lists (`CLAIM_IDENTITY_KEYS`, `NODE_IDENTITY_KEYS`, `WORK_ITEM_IDENTITY_KEYS`) are byte-identical to v264 and this phase does not read them.",
